solution_overview: "**Given:** $A = \\{1,2,3,4,5\\}$, $B = \\{3,4,5,6,7\\}$, and $C = \\{5,6,7,8,9\\}$.\n\n**Rule:** $X \\cap Y$ (**intersection**) keeps the elements that appear in both sets. $X \\cup Y$ (**union**) collects every element of either set, each written once. $X \\setminus Y$ (**set difference**) keeps the elements of $X$ that are not in $Y$. Two sets are **disjoint** when their intersection is empty.\n\n**Work:**\n\n**Step 1.** Intersect $A$ and $B$. Test the elements of $A$ one by one: $1 \\notin B$ and $2 \\notin B$, but $3, 4, 5 \\in B$. So $A \\cap B = \\{3,4,5\\}$.\n\n**Step 2.** Intersect that answer with $C$. Of $3, 4, 5$, only $5$ appears in $C$, so $(A \\cap B) \\cap C = \\{5\\}$.\n\n**Step 3.** Union $A$ and $C$. Write every number that appears in at least one of the two lists: $A \\cup C = \\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9\\}$.\n\n**Step 4.** Subtract $C$ from $A$. Delete from $A$ anything that also lies in $C$; only $5$ qualifies, so $A \\setminus C = \\{1,2,3,4\\}$.\n\n**Step 5.** Intersect $B$ and $C$. The numbers $5, 6, 7$ appear in both lists, so $B \\cap C = \\{5,6,7\\}$, which is not empty.\n\n**Results:** $A \\cap B = \\{3,4,5\\}$; $(A \\cap B) \\cap C = \\{5\\}$; $A \\cup C = \\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9\\}$; $A \\setminus C = \\{1,2,3,4\\}$; $B \\cap C = \\{5,6,7\\} \\neq \\emptyset$.",
