solution_overview: "**Given:** $A = \\{a,b,c\\}$, a set with three objects in it.\n\n**Rule:** $x \\in A$ means $x$ is one of the objects listed inside $A$. $S \\subseteq A$ (**subset**) means every object inside $S$ is also listed inside $A$. A **proper subset** must additionally be unequal to $A$. The **power set** $\\mathcal P(A)$ is the set of all subsets of $A$, and a set with $n$ elements has $2^n$ subsets.\n\n**Work:**\n\n**Step 1.** List the elements of $A$. They are the three objects $a$, $b$, $c$. Note that none of them is a set, so $\\lvert A \\rvert = 3$.\n\n**Step 2.** Count the subsets. Building a subset means deciding include or exclude for each of the three objects, which gives $2 \\times 2 \\times 2 = 2^3 = 8$ subsets.\n\n**Step 3.** Write them out to be sure:\n\n$$\\mathcal P(A)=\\{\\emptyset,\\{a\\},\\{b\\},\\{c\\},\\{a,b\\},\\{a,c\\},\\{b,c\\},A\\}.$$\n\n**Step 4.** Check the two special subsets. $\\emptyset \\subseteq A$ because it has no element that could break the rule, and $A \\subseteq A$ because every element of $A$ is in $A$. But $A$ is not a *proper* subset of itself, since proper inclusion also demands $A \\neq A$, which is false.\n\n**Step 5.** Pick out the two-element subsets from Step 3: $\\{a,b\\}$, $\\{a,c\\}$, $\\{b,c\\}$, so there are $\\binom{3}{2} = 3$ of them.\n\n**Results:** $\\lvert \\mathcal P(A) \\rvert = 8$; the elements of $A$ are only $a$, $b$, $c$; $\\emptyset \\subseteq A$; $A$ is not a proper subset of $A$; there are exactly $3$ two-element subsets.",
