 solution_overview: "**Given:** Petra, Quinn, Ravi, Sana and Theo are assigned to review a paper. 1. If Petra reviews, then Quinn does not. 2. Ravi reviews unless Sana reviews. 3. If Quinn does not review, then Theo reviews. 4. At least one of Petra or Sana reviews. 5. If Theo reviews, then Ravi does not. 6. Sana reviews only if Petra reviews.\n\n**Rule:** In symbols: (1) Petra $\\Rightarrow \\neg$ Quinn; (2) Ravi $\\lor$ Sana; (3) $\\neg$ Quinn $\\Rightarrow$ Theo; (4) Petra $\\lor$ Sana; (5) Theo $\\Rightarrow \\neg$ Ravi; (6) Sana $\\Rightarrow$ Petra, with **contrapositive** $\\neg$ Petra $\\Rightarrow \\neg$ Sana. A valid assignment must satisfy all six rules at once.\n\n**Work:**\n\nStep 1. Test \"Petra does not review\". The contrapositive of rule (6) then removes Sana as well. But rule (4) needs Petra or Sana, and both are gone, so the rule fails.\n\nStep 2. Step 1 collapses, so Petra reviews in every valid assignment.\n\nStep 3. Rule (1), with Petra in, puts Quinn out.\n\nStep 4. Rule (3), triggered by Quinn being out, puts Theo in.\n\nStep 5. Rule (5), triggered by Theo being in, puts Ravi out.\n\nStep 6. Rule (2) needs Ravi or Sana, and Ravi is out, so Sana is in. Rule (6) is satisfied because Petra reviews.\n\nStep 7. The assignment is therefore Petra, Sana and Theo reviewing, with Quinn and Ravi not reviewing. Every status was forced by Steps 2–6, with no free choice anywhere, so this assignment is the only one.\n\n**Results:**\n\n- Petra reviews in every valid assignment (Step 1 shows the alternative fails rule 4).\n- Quinn $\\Rightarrow \\neg$ Petra is the contrapositive of rule (1), so it always holds; in fact Quinn never reviews.\n- Ravi never reviews: the chain Petra in $\\to$ Quinn out $\\to$ Theo in $\\to$ Ravi out fires every time.\n- Theo reviews (rule 3) and Sana reviews (rule 2 with Ravi out).\n- The assignment {Petra, Sana, Theo} is unique, since every status is forced.",
